From a0981a4701085d89825f23f2298e6df30317935f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 01:07:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] settings: the resolutions of the display replaced in one
 step, under the lock

CDisplaySettings::GetResolutionInfo() holds the lock of the list (m_critical)
only to find an entry, and gives a reference that its caller reads after the
lock is let go. Those callers run on other threads too: a JSON-RPC request or
the options of a setting (the fillers of videoscreen.resolution, screenmode
and whitelist, GetResolutionFromString(), GetStringFromResolution()), an
add-on that asks for System.ScreenResolution (CGraphicContext::GetResInfo()).
A list made again meanwhile the way every window system makes it,
ClearCustomResolutions() (which took no lock at all) and then
AddResolutionInfo() for each resolution (which can move the entries to grow
the vector), frees or moves the entry that such a reference names. On GBM the
list was made once, at the start; now that it is made again while Kodi runs
(when the display changed), that can happen.

GBM's UpdateResolutions() makes the list whole and hands it to
CDisplaySettings::ReplaceCustomResolutions(), which, under the lock, makes the
new list apart (the entries before RES_CUSTOM as they were, each resolution as
AddResolutionInfo() keeps it, RES_DESKTOP the copy of its resolution as GBM
set it), puts it in place of the one before, applies the calibrations, and
keeps the list before until Kodi ends: a reference into it stays valid (of the
list before), and no reader sees a list half made
(settings/ResolutionListReplace.h). The list is the one made the way before,
entry by entry. ResolutionInfoSize() and ClearCustomResolutions() take the
lock now too (the other window systems still use the latter).

What stays as it was, in Kodi's code for every platform: an entry written in
place by the application thread (a calibration, ResetScreenParameters(), the
"GetResolutionInfo(RES_DESKTOP) = ..." of the other window systems) while
another thread copies it; and a reader that walks the list
(ResolutionInfoSize(), then each entry) may see the list before up to some
entry and the new one after it, now without reading freed memory.

Host test (outside the tree): the list that the header makes is the one of the
way before in 5000 random cases, and the list before is kept as it was; four
threads read entries as the callers of GetResolutionInfo() do while one makes
the list again 20000 times: clean under AddressSanitizer and ThreadSanitizer.
The way before, in the same test: heap-use-after-free (AddressSanitizer) and
data races (ThreadSanitizer).
---
